## Why This Is Asked
This tests advanced spatial indexing with dynamic updates and order statistics in 3D, relevant for large-scale simulations (NVIDIA) or online marketplaces with spatial constraints (Instacart, Amazon).

## Key Concepts
- 3D range trees and nested segment trees
- Weight compression and Fenwick trees for counts/sums
- Order statistics via binary search over weight indices
- Handling duplicates via multiplicities per weight index
- Fractional cascading for faster multi-node queries

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton: define core structures and update/query stubs
class Fenwick {
  constructor(size) {
    this.size = size;
    this.tree = new Array(size + 1).fill(0);
  }

  update(index, delta) {
    for (let i = index; i <= this.size; i += i & -i) {
      this.tree[i] += delta;
    }
  }

  query(index) {
    let sum = 0;
    for (let i = index; i > 0; i -= i & -i) {
      sum += this.tree[i];
    }
    return sum;
  }
}

class Dynamic3DPointSet {
  constructor(points) {
    this.weightCompressor = new Map();
    this.xSegmentTree = null;
    // Initialize data structures
  }

  insertPoint(x, y, z, w) {
    const weightIndex = this.compressedWeight(w);
    this.updateAlongXPath(x, y, z, weightIndex, +1);
  }

  erasePoint(x, y, z, w) {
    const weightIndex = this.compressedWeight(w);
    this.updateAlongXPath(x, y, z, weightIndex, -1);
  }

  sumWeightsInBox(x1, x2, y1, y2, z1, z2) {
    return this.rangeQuery(x1, x2, y1, y2, z1, z2, 'sum');
