## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates knowledge of practical data-structure trade-offs for dynamic ranking. Candidates should justify when to sort on demand vs. maintain auxiliary structures.

## Key Concepts
- Hash map for O(1) key access
- On-demand sorting for top-k retrieval
- Trade-off: simpler updates vs. slower top-k retrieval

## Code Example
```javascript
class CounterTopK {
  constructor() {
    this.counts = new Map(); // key -> count
  }
  add(key, delta) {
    const cur = this.counts.get(key) || 0;
    const next = cur + delta;
    if (next <= 0) this.counts.delete(key); else this.counts.set(key, next);
  }
  get(key) { return this.counts.get(key) || 0; }
  topK(k) {
    const arr = Array.from(this.counts.entries());
    arr.sort((a,b)=> b[1]-a[1] || a[0].localeCompare(b[0]));
    return arr.slice(0,k).map(([key]) => key);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests expertise in fully dynamic Li Chao trees, offline lifetime decomposition, and practical handling of duplicates in production settings.

## Key Concepts
- **Segment tree over time** for lifetime interval decomposition
- **Per-node Li Chao trees** for minimum query aggregation
- **Path-query traversal** across O(log N) nodes
- Memory-time trade-offs for handling large N and duplicates

## Code Example
```javascript
class LiChao {
  insert(line) { /* insert line into this node's Li Chao tree */ }
  query(x) { /* return minimum y at x across stored lines */ }
}

class DynamicLineSet {
  constructor(N) {
    this.segmentTree = new SegmentTree(N);
  }

  addLine(m, b, id, insertTime) {
    const interval = [insertTime, Infinity];
    this.segmentTree.addInterval(interval, { m, b, id });
  }

  removeLine(id, removeTime) {
    // Update the line's interval end time in the segment tree
    this.segmentTree.closeInterval(id, removeTime);
  }

  queryMin(x, queryTime) {
    return this.segmentTree.queryPath(queryTime)
      .reduce((min, lichao) => Math.min(min, lichao.query(x)), Infinity);
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
| Operation | Time | Space |
|-----------|------|-------|
| addLine | O(log N) | O(log N) per line |
| removeLine | O(log N) | - |
| queryMin | O(log² N) | - |

## Edge Cases
- **Identical slopes**: Stored separately with unique ids; Li Chao tree handles min correctly
- **Immediate removal**: Assigned to leaf node only
- **Large N**: Memory scales with O(N log N) total line-node assignments

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design an order-statistics structure with a global offset that shifts all values, while correctly handling duplicates and mapping erasures to the current offset.

## Key Concepts
- Order-statistics BST (treap/AVL with size augmentation)
- Global lazy offset for all elements
- Handling duplicates via per-key counts
- Correct mapping for erase when offset changes

## Code Example
